>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `register_instance`

``` hol4
linarithData.register_instance :
  linarithData.linarith_instance -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Installs a carrier implementation in the session-local linarith registry.

A linarith instance is one carrier type's implementation of everything
the generic engine needs: the type itself, whether its order is discrete,
term destructors and a literal constructor, the theorems replayed for
addition, scaling, negated order, disequality and non-negativity, a
normalising conversion, and the carrier rewrites, operator-splitting
rules and per-atom facts it contributes to preprocessing. Registration
makes these available to decomposition, preprocessing and certificate
replay for the whole session, so that no part of the engine tests for a
particular carrier. Injections between carriers are installed separately
with `register_injection`.

The contract of each field — including which of them may decline an
individual term and which merely record that the carrier has no such
operator — is documented on the `linarith_instance` type in
`src/auto/linarith/linarithData.sig`, which is the authority on it.

The registry is keyed by `#ty`. Registering another instance for the same
type atomically replaces the old entry and issues a warning. Registration
does not eagerly validate the theorem kit; malformed components fail when
an arithmetic operation consumes them. Instance modules normally register
their records as a module-loading side effect.

### Failure

Registration itself performs no theorem-kit validation. Invalid callbacks
or replay theorems generally fail only when the instance is used.

### Example

The natural-number instance can be reinstalled explicitly. Calling the
resulting function after `linarithLib` has loaded intentionally warns that
it replaces the existing entry.

``` hol4
fun reinstall_num_instance () =
  linarithData.register_instance linarithNum.instance;
```

